.TH CG 1 "October 2026" "cg 1.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cg \- fast multi-threaded POSIX/GNU-style grep implementation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cg
.RB [ OPTION ]...
.I PATTERNS
.RB [ FILE ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cg
searches each input file for selected lines matching one or more patterns.
If no file is given, standard input is read. A file name of
.B -
also means standard input.
.PP
The implementation uses specialized fixed-string scanners, a Thompson-NFA /
lazy-DFA engine for its supported regular-expression subset, and the system
regular-expression implementation as a fallback for patterns requiring features
outside the internal engine. Large files can be processed by multiple workers.
.SH PATTERN SELECTION AND INTERPRETATION
.TP
.BR -E , " --extended-regexp"
Interpret patterns as extended regular expressions.
.TP
.BR -F , " --fixed-strings"
Interpret patterns as fixed strings. This is the primary high-throughput path.
.TP
.BR -G , " --basic-regexp"
Interpret patterns as basic regular expressions. This is the default.
.TP
.BI "-e " PATTERNS , " --regexp=" PATTERNS
Use PATTERNS for matching. May be specified more than once.
.TP
.BI "-f " FILE , " --file=" FILE
Read patterns from FILE, one pattern per line. A FILE of
.B -
reads patterns from standard input.
.TP
.BR -i , " --ignore-case"
Ignore case distinctions. The
.B -y
option is also accepted as a synonym.
.TP
.BR --no-ignore-case
Do not ignore case distinctions.
.TP
.BR -w , " --word-regexp"
Select only lines containing matches that form whole words.
.TP
.BR -x , " --line-regexp"
Select only lines that match a complete pattern.
.SH OUTPUT CONTROL
.TP
.BR -c , " --count"
Print only the count of selected lines for each file.
.TP
.BR -n , " --line-number"
Prefix output lines with their line number.
.TP
.BR -b , " --byte-offset"
Prefix output with the byte offset.
.TP
.BR -H , " --with-filename"
Always print the file name with selected output.
.TP
.BR -h , " --no-filename"
Suppress the file name prefix.
.TP
.BR -l , " --files-with-matches"
Print only names of files containing a selected line.
.TP
.BR -L , " --files-without-match"
Print only names of files containing no selected lines.
.TP
.BR -o , " --only-matching"
Show only the nonempty parts of lines that match.
.TP
.BR -q , " --quiet , --silent"
Suppress normal output. Exit status still indicates whether a match occurred.
.TP
.BR -s , " --no-messages"
Suppress error messages.
.TP
.BI "-m " NUM , " --max-count=" NUM
Stop after NUM selected lines per file.
.TP
.BR -Z , " --null"
Print a NUL byte after file names.
.TP
.BI "--color[=WHEN]"
Highlight matches using markers. WHEN may be
.BR always ,
.BR never , or
.BR auto .
The spelling
.B --colour
is also accepted.
.TP
.BI "--line-buffered"
Flush output after every line.
.TP
.BI "--label=" LABEL
Use LABEL as the name for standard input in output.
.SH CONTEXT
.TP
.BI "-A " NUM , " --after-context=" NUM
Print NUM lines of trailing context.
.TP
.BI "-B " NUM , " --before-context=" NUM
Print NUM lines of leading context.
.TP
.BI "-C " NUM , " --context=" NUM
Print NUM lines of context before and after selected lines.
.TP
.BI - NUM
A decimal option such as
.B -3
is accepted as shorthand for
.B --context=3.
.SH FILE TRAVERSAL
.TP
.BR -r , " --recursive"
Recursively search directories without following symbolic links.
.TP
.BR -R , " --dereference-recursive"
Recursively search while following symbolic links.
.TP
.BI "-d " ACTION , " --directories=" ACTION
Handle directories using
.BR read ,
.BR recurse , or
.BR skip .
.TP
.BI "-D " ACTION , " --devices=" ACTION
Accept
.BR read
or
.BR skip
for compatibility.
.TP
.BI "--include=" GLOB
Search only files whose names match GLOB.
.TP
.BI "--exclude=" GLOB
Skip files whose names match GLOB.
.TP
.BI "--exclude-dir=" GLOB
Skip directories whose names match GLOB.
.SH BINARY INPUT
.TP
.BR -a , " --text"
Treat binary input as text.
.TP
.BR -I
Treat binary files as without-match.
.TP
.BI "--binary-files=" TYPE
TYPE is one of
.BR binary ,
.BR text , or
.BR without-match .
.SH PERFORMANCE AND THREADING
.TP
.BI "-j " N , " --threads=" N
Use N worker threads. N must be between 1 and 256. The default on Linux is
the detected physical-core count.
.PP
Large files are memory-mapped and divided into work chunks. The environment
variable
.B CG_CHUNK_KIB
can tune the work-unit size from 64 KiB through 1 GiB. The default is about
4 MiB.
.PP
On x86-64, several literal-search paths use AVX2 when the relevant implementation
path is selected. Performance is workload- and CPU-dependent.
.SH REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
The internal regex engine supports a defined BRE/ERE subset. Patterns requiring
features outside that engine can be passed to the system regex implementation.
The source also recognizes pure literal alternatives before building the NFA so
large literal alternations can remain on the specialized literal path.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one selected line was found. With
.B -q,
this is returned as soon as a match is established.
.TP
.B 1
No selected lines were found.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred, including an explicitly unsupported option.
.SH UNSUPPORTED OPTIONS
The following options are explicitly rejected by the current implementation:
.TP
.B -P, --perl-regexp
Perl-compatible regular expressions are not supported.
.TP
.B -z, --null-data
NUL-separated input records are not supported.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CG_CHUNK_KIB
Controls the large-file worker chunk size. Values outside 64 KiB through 1 GiB
are ignored.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cg -F needle file.txt
Search for a fixed string.
.TP
.B cg -F -f patterns.txt large.txt
Search for many fixed strings read from a pattern file.
.TP
.B cg -E 'foo|bar' file.txt
Search using an extended regular expression.
.TP
.B cg -j 8 -F -f patterns.txt large.txt
Use eight worker threads.
.TP
.B cg -r -F needle src/
Recursively search a directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1)
.SH NOTES
The command-line interface is intentionally grep-like but is not a claim of full
GNU grep compatibility. Consult the project's compatibility documentation and
run
.B cg --help
for the authoritative option list for the installed build.
.SH AUTHOR
Project author and maintainer: see the project repository.
